---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Task Breakdown - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="task-breakdown">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        In this exercise, you will create a <strong>Task Breakdown Skill</strong> to automate the planning phase of development. <i>Recall:</i> the role of the task breakdown skill is the tech lead who turns specs into work items. You use this skill to generate implementation plans for the remaining features of the dashboard.
      </p>

      <!-- 1. Review Existing Task Breakdowns -->
      <section>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">1. Review Existing Task Breakdowns</h3>
        <p>
          Before teaching the AI how to break down tasks, it helps to see what "good" looks like. We've included example task breakdowns in the sample project documentation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Take a moment to read through the existing tasks found in: <code>docs/tasks</code>
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice the patterns in these files:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
          <li><strong>Dependency Awareness:</strong> Tasks list what must be done first.</li>
          <li><strong>Links to Requirements:</strong> Each task ties back to the feature goal.</li>
          <li><strong>Implementable Steps:</strong> The description gives a developer clear direction.</li>
          <li><strong>Acceptance Criteria:</strong> Acceptance criteria act as a checklist for determining the expected “done” state.</li>
        </ul>

        <Callout variant="info" title="Why this matters">
          <p>
            When you create your skill, you'll point the AI to these examples. This is the <strong>golden master</strong> technique: showing is better than telling.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. Create a Custom Task Breakdown Skill -->
      <section>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">2. Create a Custom Task Breakdown Skill</h3>
        <p>
          Now, use the <strong>skill-creator</strong> (which you installed in the previous step) to build your new skill.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-4">
          You want a skill that generates task breakdowns for new features. It should use the patterns from the example tasks you just reviewed.
        </p>

        <TerminalBlock
            title="Example Prompt"
            lines={[
                {type: "command", command: "Create a task breakdown skill for generating task breakdowns for new features. The skill should: define clear tasks, track dependencies between tasks, list files created or modified at each step, link tasks back to requirements, produce clear acceptance criteria, and give tasks scope estimation. Base the output format on the examples found in @docs/tasks/"}
            ]}
        />

        <p class="mt-4">
          Provide as much context as possible to the skill creator. The more it knows about your preferences now, the less you'll have to correct it later.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Review and Improve the Generated Skill -->
      <section>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">3. Review and Improve the Generated Skill</h3>
        <p>
          Once the skill is generated, open the created <code>SKILL.md</code> file (likely in <code>.agents/skills/task-breakdown/</code> or similar).
        </p>

        <p>
          Review the generated instructions. Does it capture the nuances you care about?
        </p>

        <p>
          For guidance on what a robust task breakdown skill should contain, refer to:
        </p>
        <div class="my-4">
          <a href="/task-breakdowns/task-breakdown-skills" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 underline font-medium">
            Task Breakdown Skills
          </a>
        </div>

        <p>
          That page explains the anatomy of a good skill, including how to structure dependencies and handle sizing conventions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Add Personal Preferences -->
      <section>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">4. Add Personal Preferences</h3>
        <p>
          This is your skill - customize it to fit how you and your team work. If your team has specific workflows or preferences, feel free to define them here.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider adding specific instructions to the <code>SKILL.md</code> file, such as:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
          <li><strong>Output Structure:</strong> "Format the output as a checklist I can copy into PR descriptions."</li>
          <li><strong>Sizing:</strong> "Keep all tasks under 2 hours of estimated effort."</li>
        </ul>

        <Callout variant="tip" title="Iterate">
          <p>
            You can modify the skill manually or ask the AI to update it: "Update the task breakdown skill to always include a 'Files to Modify' section."
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Generate Task Overviews Using the Skill -->
      <section>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">5. Generate Task Overviews Using the Skill</h3>
        <p>
          Use the task breakdown skill to create task breakdowns for the remaining dashboard features. Since the skill already exists, and you have created comprehensive requirements docs for outstanding features, the final prompt requires far less detail.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 mb-2">
          <strong>Outstanding Features:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600 mb-4">
          <li><strong>Notepad:</strong> A quick scratchpad widget.</li>
          <li><strong>Pomodoro Timer:</strong> A focus timer.</li>
          <li><strong>Habit Tracker:</strong> A habit tracker widget.</li>
          <li>The new <strong>unit converter widget</strong> or custom feature of your choice.</li>
        </ul>

        <TerminalBlock
            title="Example Prompt"
            lines={[
                { type: "command", command: "Using the task breakdown skill, generate a comprehensive task overview for implementing the Pomodoro Timer feature based on the requirements in: @docs/requirements/pomodoro-timer.md."}
            ]}
        />

        <p class="mt-4">
          Review the output. Are the tasks clear? Are they implementable? If not, refine your skill or adjust the tasks manually. Don't expect to build a perfect skill on the first attempt - this will likely require multiple iterations with tweaks to get the best output.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-stone-200 pt-8 mt-8">
        <p class="text-stone-800 font-medium">
          By automating this planning step, you ensure every feature starts with a solid plan, without having to write it all from scratch every time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
